[s0l0!-header][h1]Array Destructuring in ES6[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]destructuring [/b]assignment syntax is a JavaScript expression that makes it possible to unpack values from arrays, or properties from objects, into distinct variables. 
ES6 has added a shorthand syntax for destructuring an array.

The following example demonstrates how to unpack the elements of an array into distinct variables: [code format="js" codeId="1203"]let arr = ['1', '2', '3'];
[b]let [one, two, three] = arr;[/b]

console.log(one); // 1
console.log(two); // 2
console.log(three); // 3[/code]
We can use also destructure an array returned by a function.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1204"]let a = () => {
  return [1, 3, 2];
};

[b]let [one, , two] = a();[/b][/code]
Notice that we left the second argument's place empty.

The destructuring syntax also simplifies assignment and swapping values: [code format="js" codeId="1205"]let a, b, c = 4, d = 8;
[a, b = 6] = [2]; // a = 2, b = 6

[c, d] = [d, c]; // c = 8, d = 4[/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

