[s0l0!-header][h1]Object Destructuring in ES6[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Similar to Array destructuring, [b]Object destructuring[/b] unpacks properties into distinct variables.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="js" codeId="1578"]let obj = {h:100, s: true};
[b]let {h, s} = obj;[/b]

console.log(h); // 100
console.log(s); // true[/code]
We can assign without declaration, but there are some syntax requirements for that: [code format="js" codeId="1579"]let a, b;
({a, b} = {a: 'Hello ', b: 'Jack'});

console.log(a + b); // Hello Jack[/code]
The [b]()[/b] with a [b]semicolon (;)[/b] at the end are [b]mandatory[/b] when destructuring without a declaration. However, you can also do it as follows where the [b]()[/b] are not required: [code format="js" codeId="1580"]let {a, b} = {a: 'Hello ', b: 'Jack'};
console.log(a + b);[/code]
You can also assign the object to new variable names.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1581"]var o = {h: 42, s: true};
[b]var {h: foo, s: bar} = o;[/b]

//console.log(h); // Error
console.log(foo); // 42[/code]
Finally you can assign [b]default values[/b] to variables, in case the value unpacked from the object is undefined.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1582"]var obj = {id: 42, name: "Jack"};

[b]let {id = 10, age = 20} = obj;[/b]

console.log(id); // 42
console.log(age); // 20[/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

